ister  appointed nadimus arway as  a vaccine minister to make sure we did  everything we can globally to iron out  supply chains  um talking as well just finally on the  vaccine we've seen  a difficult week for astrazeneca uh  european countries  pausing the rollout many of them  restarting it and of course this comes  off the back of  for example the french president  describing it as quasi-effective  do you think some of this criticism of  the astrazeneca  vaccine is political rather than being  based on science  well i think what it shows and you know  as defence secretary uh  sort of here to hopefully launch our  defense reforms  uh what it shows is that in the world we  live in the global world we live in  information is a very very important  tool and we have to make sure that  as politicians and as governments of  country we are responsible with how we  use that information and that we are  how's the french president been  responsible well  i'll let the media be a judge of that my  view is that the  european medical agency has approved  astrazeneca to be safe  a number of countries that said they  were going to pause it have now done a  uter  and are starting to roll it out i think  it's really for the governments of each  of those european countries  to answer the questions of their own  citizens about why or when they're going  to get their vaccines  okay now as you say you are defense  secretary uh you're publishing the  defense command paper  tomorrow now there are reports the army  is going to be cut  by 10 000 troops as part of that is that  true  well i'm not going to reveal on on the  media before parliament i'm afraid the  details  of numbers of our men and women other  armed forces you know  it's important that i deliver the  command paper parliament establishes the  armed forces  and that is my obligation to parliament  so i'm not going to  get into the speculation we had in  november a speculative story i remember  in the bbc about  cutting the army to 65 000. i've seen  lots of numbers used  i think the assurance i can give the  viewers is that you know what i will be  doing is making sure we have an armed  forces it's the right size  to meet the threat and the right size to  meet the government's ambition  of having a global britain that can  uphold values and support its allies  and you're launching this new spy ship  what's the idea  well i think back in november the prime  minister announced a a multi-role  surveillance vessel i i don't call it a  spy ship i mean obviously what's key  here is to make sure that we  protect our critical lash infrastructure  what we what we have seen is that our  adversaries have spotted  that some of our weaknesses are  obviously different from what they were  10 20 years ago  we are very very dependent on things  like cables and satellites in space  and if we don't invest in the  capabilities to protect those  then we will be left deeply vulnerable  and there's lots of media reporting  around  some russian programmes that we have  seen about looking at that  and therefore it's my duty as defense  centrally to make sure we invest in  capabilities to make sure the lights  don't go out at home  are you talking a lot of course about a  sort of changing world and sort of new  threats one of the biggest challenges  of course is how to deal with china  now this week your integrated defense  review called for a positive  trade and investment relationship with  china  in light of china's actions in hong kong  towards the uyghur muslims  shouldn't we be a bit tougher than that  well i think we have we are certainly  tougher than previous governments we are  putting in place new legislation to make  sure that's not hard when you look at  david cameron george osborne  what i would say is we are you know  putting in place laws to make sure that  we  the government can protect our security  and our businesses  from necessarily from being bought up  potentially by chinese companies  that would seek to take away that  intellectual property uh the  foreign secretary only a few weeks ago  set out a range of measures  to look at companies uh that may trade  when in the region of china where the  uyghur are being systematically and  clearly persecuted  and we have over time called out members  of the chinese government who've been  involved in cyber crime and  cyber espionage you know and not only  that we've also raised it in the u.n and  asked for  uh you know investigations and further  work on  uh calling out this behavior so they're  calling out those just to come in  sorry just to come in because some  people listening to you will say calling  out that's just not enough is it just  calling somebody out china's not gonna  care about that you want to have a  positive trade and investment  relationship this is a country that's  holding weakest in camps that there are  reports of women being forcibly  sterilized children being taken away  and left in orphanages is the position  effectively that if you're  wealthy enough for country if you're an  important enough trading partner then we  won't do anything else other than just  say a few words that calling you out  no sophie look first of all we live in  the world we live in not in the world we  necessarily  are at or wish to be and on that route  to get there what we need to make sure  is that we use all the  the tools we have at our disposal but  you know british companies up and down  this country  trade with china their customers go into  shops and buy things  from products that are manufacturing  china and yes we want to do more to  encourage  british manufacturing and more to  diversify that supply chain because that  is ultimately the best way  to get stronger leverage on china to get  their behavior to change which is  to be a less dependent on them but also  use those levers and i think  my point of view would be if we didn't  trade with china we would actually have  in some areas less levers to try and  influence china's behavior  you know the international community  alongside the united states  and others and in the u.n know that if  we want  china's behavior to change we think it  is appalling what's going on with the  weaker  but you know we cannot just overnight  stop uh trading directly with china what  we can do is take steps to make sure  that  the trade we do is restricted that the  trade  you know we have an arms embargo on  china for example  and that we take steps we are wide-eyed  about  china's place in the world but china's  place in the world is significantly  uh large and and you know again as i  said you know  over the years people have had a  different relationship with china we are  effectively resetting that making it  very clear the parameters  and taking steps where we can okay  um ethiopia a huge issue  at the moment a really devastating  situation four months of war half a  million people  lost their homes what is the uk doing  well first of all the united nations  we've uh called that out a  lead led calling out of that we've also  the ambassador asked  and called upon the forces there to  de-escalate  and we're horrified by what we've seen i  think we're going to investigate that  further  and the ambassador on the ground has  been very clear we call upon all the  parties  to step away and you know it's just it's  just horrendous what's been going on and  and that's why you know at the un and  obviously we are chairing the security  council at the moment in the united  nations making sure  that the united nations and the  international community come come along  to make sure that this just doesn't get  any worse and in fact we deal with those  people  that have done those things and one more  very quick question while i have you uh  reports today that refugees are going to  be granted  indefinitely to remain in the uk to try  and encourage  more legal routes what's the idea  well i the home secretary will obviously  announce the details next week when the  bill is  launched at parliament and i don't think  i would uh speculate on on some of the  press reports about  legislation before it is put forward or  need white papers to govern but i think  i think first and foremost we recognize  that the asylum seeker  is not working well and we have to deal  with that we have to also deal with the  people traffickers that  bring people across the channel they are  wicked people uh doing some horrendous  things  and you know it is not in a place we  want it to be and and so we've got to  work with it we also recognize however  that you know conflict drives a lot of  this migration which is why  you know as defense secretary i have to  you know make it very clear to people  that  defense is not only in the business if  it has to of hard power such as  you know fighting a war if that's what  is required to defend this nation but  also in the business of conflict  prevention  and you talk about the issues around for  example ethiopia you know we're in sudan  we're in somalia  over the years as the british armed  forces because security is often  first and foremost what is most  important to preventing  those refugee flows in the first place  okay  thank you so much for being on the  program today ben wallace there thank  you
 

